{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity","id":1144637,"title":"Proposed Fix for 2E Toxicity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-27T22:30:40-07:00","id":1144637,"title":"Proposed Fix for 2E Toxicity","content":"I tried to make a post about this a while back, but I was a condescending douche and nobody understood what I was saying. I'll try to be more respectful and coherent this time.<br /> <br /> I don't like the 2E update to the Toxicity rules. As a quick recap: in 1E resisting poison was Stam + Res - Tox and success was winner-take all; in 2E, the roll is the same but successes reduce damage by 1 each from damage = Toxicity. I understand this was partly due to a desire to make Resisted rolls uniformly &quot;successes matter&quot; rather than &quot;success matters.&quot; Further complicating matters are Armory and Hurt Locker ailments, which use their own variations of the 1E system despite being published in different editions.<br /> <br /> 2E's update is a pretty extreme increase in lethality. That's mostly because each extra point of Toxicity requires 4 dice worth of expected successes to counteract (1 for the penalty, plus 3 more to reduce 1 avg damage). Basically: the 2E rules make mid-range poisons deadly to average-stat charactes and top-range poisons completely unstoppable. They also make higher dice pools and resistance bonuses pretty useless.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TL;DR Math</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I did the napkin math using a dice probability table for Resist Poison dice pools of 2-13 (up to max for unaugmented human) and Toxicity 1-8, finding that 2E increased average damage by 50% (average DPT 2.37 vs 3.56). At 13 Resist dice, 2E poisons are almost 5x as deadly as 1E poisons. At 9 resist dice (basically 2x 3 stats + willpower), 2E poisons are almost twice as deadly.as 1E poisons. To explain what I mean by &quot;higher dice pools are useless&quot;, 2E poisons do average 4.375 DPT vs a dice pool of 2 and 2.42 DPT vs a dice pool of 13 = the best possible human spending Willpower resists only 80% more poison than the worst possible human spending nothing. For a difference worth 32 xp.<br /> <br /> For a practical example, the book lists a typical alcohol poisoning with beer or wine as a Toxicity 3 event with 8 iterations. Let's say you have a typical character with 2 in all stats, that means they have a dice pool of 4 and can use +3 Willpower 4 times. 1E rules have that char taking 0.7203 * 4 = 2.88 damage over the first 4 turns with Willpower and 2.1 * 4 = 8.4 more over the next 4. If that's 11 bashing, then that player will be REALLY hurting for the rest of the week if they're lucky or comatose if they're not. If that's 11 lethal, then they still probably won't die but they'll most probably be comatose and REALLY should be hospitalized.<br /> <br /> The same example with 2E rules? 17.52 damage. If that's bashing, you're over halfway through aggravated. If Lethal, you're full aggravated in 7 hours. Sure, drinking is bad for your health, but that's really freakin' extreme. An average American male weighing 195.7 lbs binge drinking 2.5 standard drinks per hour for 8 hours is at about .338 BAC, which is nearly lethal but survivable in most cases.<br /> <br /> I really don't want to get bogged down in arguments about the merits of binge drinking, so please don't get hung up on that. It's just that it's the *only* clear example that the core rules give other than heroin overdose (for those curious: Toxicity 7 for 8 - Stamina hours = 1E 37.8 damage vs 2E 41.4).</div> </div> </div> </div>Here's my argument: if 1E's resisted winner-takes-all rules make outcomes too lopsided and 2E's resisted damage-subtract rules are too punishing... how about you keep damage-subtract but take away the penalty to Resolve + Stamina? Stated more clearly: Resolve + Stamina vs automatic Toxicity damage, successes subtract. So if you roll Stamina 3 + Resolve 3 and get 2 successes, then you subtract 2 from whatever the Toxicity is for the actual damage.<br /> <br /> The result flattens out the damage curve and eliminates &quot;I rolled a 10 on my chance die so take no damage from VX gas!&quot; while increasing average damage by a modest 5% over 1E's rules. If you care about the binge drinking example, average damage is 10.72 and it's 30.02 for heroin. <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/open?id=0B43LUTYCZ-ZUMEZOWUpCMS1NWjg\" target=\"_blank\">All the math is on my Google Drive</a> if anyone wants to check it.<br /> <br /> What do you think? Has anyone had much use for Toxicity rules for characters and NPC's? Stories of success and failure? Any workarounds anyone else has tried? Reasons to love the 2e system? References to fixes or homebrews better than mine?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11153-bonerstorm","name":"bonerstorm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-27T22:59:44-07:00","id":1144645,"title":"","content":"Nice! I actually kind of like that change.<br /> <br /> I do, however, think you're using tl;dr wrong. Your tl;dr is longer than your actual post. (For the record I did read the whole thing, I just thought it was funny)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T03:37:03-07:00","id":1144687,"title":"","content":"It's not a bad alternate, but in my experience the 2e RAW don't match with your points. As well, your math seems off, that the linked spread sheet has the expected successes missing 10-agains, which is certainly going to devalue higher dice-pools (also the highest dice-pool without supernatural powers is 16, Stamina 5, Resolve 5, Hardy 3, Willpower).<br /> <br /> The whole &quot;bigger dice pools are useless&quot; just flatly isn't mathematically true. Someone with 10 base dice against a Toxicity of 3 eight times is clearly mathematically better off than someone with 4 base dice. 7 dice 8 times averages 18.67 successes, leaving just 5.33 left over (I'm aware I'm simplifying a bit here as it's probably that 7 dice will over roll and 'waste' some of those average successes). That's not enough to put anything capable of throwing 10 base dice into anything other than the need to take it easy for a bit.1 die 8 times averages 2.67 successes, leaving 21.33 left which... is going to kill anything with just 4 dice as a base.<br /> <br /> Of course the decrease in effectiveness of higher dice pools is more drastic the higher you go compared to 1e, but that's just baked into moving from an all or noting system to a per success system. That doesn't mean higher dice pools are &quot;worse&quot; or &quot;useless&quot; in the 2e rules.<br /> <br /> The whole &quot;average DPT&quot; part seems completely obfuscating the math instead of illuminating it.<br /> <br /> If I have a base of 10 dice:<br /> Toxicity 1: Average result 3 successes, zero damage.<br /> Toxicity 2: Average result 2.67 successes, zero damage.<br /> Toxicity 3. Average result 2.33 success, 0.67 damage.<br /> Toxicity 4. Average result 2 successes, 2 damage.<br /> Toxicity 5. Average result 1.67 successes, 3.33 damage<br /> Toxicity 6. Average result 1.33 successes, 4.67 damage<br /> Toxicity 7: Average result 1 success, 6 damage<br /> Toxicity 8: Average result 0.67 success, 7.33 damage<br /> <br /> Just saying that the average is 3 is skipping a lot of information. It's not even being compared to the average damage without reduction (4.5). Hell, you can't even call that an average, because it presumes you'll face each toxicity level at the same rate which just isn't a viable assumption in the game. If you face Toxicity 3 to 5 95% of the time (I rarely see less because it's usually not enough to really matter, and I haven't seem more come up much outside of PCs trying to max it out via supernatural powers), the reduction at higher dice pools vs low dice pools is pretty significant. Your analysis is so focused on how much harsher the new rules are compared to the old ones, that it never seems to get into how deadly the new ones are in general. This also generally ignores any medical attention. The RAW make it so that you either need to be a tough SOB or really lucky to survive ODing or severe chemical exposures without medical attention... which makes sense. If you can walk away from most things without even needing first aid, Medicine and healing powers aren't really much use.<br /> <br /> I also really don't like that idea of making Toxicity 8 less deadly than 1e; even as an artifact of the math.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T04:32:10-07:00","id":1144703,"title":"","content":"There are a number of easy ways to get toxicity 6+ as a supernat though. In first edition Changeling you could get that on a weapon for two merit dots (which was far and away the best use of points if what you wanted was raw damage). A Venombite with Potent Kith can be dishing out toxicity 6 bites at character creation. A Beast with Basiliks' Touch has Toxicity 7-9 while at low Satiety. I'm in favor of using the grave poisoned tilt wherever possible, but it can create some weird situations where poison is far more lethal outside of combat than during.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T06:50:06-07:00","id":1144742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144687#post1144687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a bad alternate, but in my experience the 2e RAW don't match with your points. As well, your math seems off, that the linked spread sheet has the expected successes missing 10-agains, which is certainly going to devalue higher dice-pools (also the highest dice-pool without supernatural powers is 16, Stamina 5, Resolve 5, Hardy 3, Willpower).<br /> <br /> The whole &quot;bigger dice pools are useless&quot; just flatly isn't mathematically true. Someone with 10 base dice against a Toxicity of 3 eight times is clearly mathematically better off than someone with 4 base dice. 7 dice 8 times averages 18.67 successes, leaving just 5.33 left over (I'm aware I'm simplifying a bit here as it's probably that 7 dice will over roll and 'waste' some of those average successes). That's not enough to put anything capable of throwing 10 base dice into anything other than the need to take it easy for a bit.1 die 8 times averages 2.67 successes, leaving 21.33 left which... is going to kill anything with just 4 dice as a base.<br /> <br /> Of course the decrease in effectiveness of higher dice pools is more drastic the higher you go compared to 1e, but that's just baked into moving from an all or noting system to a per success system. That doesn't mean higher dice pools are &quot;worse&quot; or &quot;useless&quot; in the 2e rules.<br /> <br /> The whole &quot;average DPT&quot; part seems completely obfuscating the math instead of illuminating it.<br /> <br /> ....<br /> <br /> Just saying that the average is 3 is skipping a lot of information. It's not even being compared to the average damage without reduction (4.5). Hell, you can't even call that an average, because it presumes you'll face each toxicity level at the same rate which just isn't a viable assumption in the game. If you face Toxicity 3 to 5 95% of the time (I rarely see less because it's usually not enough to really matter, and I haven't seem more come up much outside of PCs trying to max it out via supernatural powers), the reduction at higher dice pools vs low dice pools is pretty significant. Your analysis is so focused on how much harsher the new rules are compared to the old ones, that it never seems to get into how deadly the new ones are in general. This also generally ignores any medical attention. The RAW make it so that you either need to be a tough SOB or really lucky to survive ODing or severe chemical exposures without medical attention... which makes sense. If you can walk away from most things without even needing first aid, Medicine and healing powers aren't really much use.<br /> <br /> I also really don't like that idea of making Toxicity 8 less deadly than 1e; even as an artifact of the math.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry that the sheet's a bit of a mess: the blue table was my first attempt and I screwed up 10agains, which is why I added an accurate probability table on the 2nd tab. I only used the 3rd column of the blue table for 1E rules, since they're winner take all and # of successes don't matter. And, lol and behold, I totally forgot Hardy was a thing. And, yes, the math should be accurate since it's adding expected values by probability and not flat expected value by average rolls. Ex: probability of 1 + probability of 2 + probability of 3... not just &quot;average roll will be 3 so 0 damage.&quot; The sheet auto-updates so you can look at average damages for different toxicities by dice pool.<br /> <br /> Of course, higher rolls aren't *completely* useless... but the 2E system makes them worth a lot less. Again, each point of Toxicity in 2E is worth 4 points of resistance. If you look at the graph on the right, you'll see that both 1E and 2E average damage sharply curves up at higher Toxicity, but 2E's damage is about twice that of 1E for resistance dice pools of 9 (which may be even a little higher than your average player spending WP). My experimental system flattens out the damage curve while still being 20% deadlier on average than 1E. That means there's a real incentive to invest in merits like Hardy and that things like Basilisk's Touch are somewhat less win button.<br /> <br /> As for mitigating factors beyond that: medical treatment doesn't give bonuses vs poison beyond +1/+2 (not a teamwork roll) and, before anyone else says it, you can't heal poison damage while still suffering the ailment. If you really dive into the sheet, you'll see the main effect of 2E poison rules is that 3-5 Tox poisons are death sentences for most players and 6+ poisons are death sentences for maxed-out demigods. My fix retains the deadliness of poison, while making sure that resistance scales because of the flat damage curve.<br /> <br /> Final note: the fix does NOT make poison less deadly than 1E. It increases lethality by 4% on average and is over twice as lethal at resist dice pools of 11+. It only makes high Tox poisons slightly less deadly for mid-range characters because they can shave a point or two off of damage instead of just rolling chance dies (which I think is more fun anyway).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11153-bonerstorm","name":"bonerstorm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T06:56:28-07:00","id":1144745,"title":"","content":"Regardless: in my fix rules nobody's walking away unscathed from a Tox 7 heroin overdose (like 1E) nor do they get instagibbed by binging on Keystone Ice (2E). It just means that tougher characters have slightly more time to get Narcan or experience real body wreckage if they don't get their stomach pumped.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11153-bonerstorm","name":"bonerstorm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T07:06:47-07:00","id":1144749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144703#post1144703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are a number of easy ways to get toxicity 6+ as a supernat though. In first edition Changeling you could get that on a weapon for two merit dots (which was far and away the best use of points if what you wanted was raw damage). A Venombite with Potent Kith can be dishing out toxicity 6 bites at character creation. A Beast with Basiliks' Touch has Toxicity 7-9 while at low Satiety. I'm in favor of using the grave poisoned tilt wherever possible, but it can create some weird situations where poison is far more lethal outside of combat than during.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much this. I'm running a crossover and use source rules for all of my mobs. This venture was inspired by one which is a Lair 4 + Stamina 2 Namtaru and I thought &quot;Damn... if she literally touches one of my PC's with a Low Satiety Basilisk's Touch, that is 100% unavoidable death for any one of them. She doesn't even have to spend Satiety!&quot; It's still really rough in my fix, but 4 avg DPT for a dice pool of 9 is a damn sight better than 6.33 for 2E rules.<br /> <br /> I see a lot of &quot;just use grave poisoned tilt&quot; on the forums when people talk about Toxicity in 2E and that really drives home for me that RAW is broken.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11153-bonerstorm","name":"bonerstorm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T13:57:50-07:00","id":1144928,"title":"","content":"Taking a completely different tack on poison rules, here's a variant I've been tinkering with: All poisons deal 1 damage per round (assuming combat or similarly high stress situations, use longer intervals where appropriate) You roll stamina (+hardy if you have it, do not add resolve) as an extended action, making one roll per round. Success(es) on the roll downgrades the damage from lethal to bashing for that turn and adds to your total. Toxicity is the target number of successes, when met you stop taking damage from the poison. <br /> <br /> It's definitely more forgiving than the current system, I'm still looking at tweaks to keep poison dangerous but not have it be the death sentence it is under the current RAW","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T16:25:03-07:00","id":1145002,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144703#post1144703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are a number of easy ways to get toxicity 6+ as a supernat though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is acknowledged in my post?<br /> <br /> Though really we don't have that many examples from 2e yet. CtL 2e isn't out yet, and it looked like Potent Kith might not go to 5 (given how insanely powerful that can be) any more, so we'll see about that. Basilik's Touch is the only current 2e power that can really dish out the Toxicity... but Atavisms are pretty nasty across the board. I've seen an Eshmaki reliably wreck opponents via From the Shadows for sneak attacks + Limb from Limb combined with some fighting Merits. At low Satiety even at Lair 1 it isn't hard to take out non-physically oriented opponents, or leave anything without good regeneration double Leg Wracked, Stunned, and in for a very rough next round. Plus they can spend Satiety to do Agg if they're dealing with something that has good bashing/lethal regen (like an angry Uratha).<br /> <br /> On the other side, 2e has a lot of decent anti-toxin powers too. Uratha automatically downgrade by Primal Urge, and if they're hit with somethiing like Basilik's Touch then can go Gauru and heal all the damage every turn (though then you get into stats and expenditures for length vs time limit, assuming the Uratha doesn't just accept Kuruth to not care). Life mages can deal with toxins pretty damned easily (Life 2 can permanent downgrade the severity by Potency, and Life 3 can heal any damage they take, and you don't need every Mage to be good at it, just one). We've seen at least one Kith in the 2e previews that downgrades severity by Wyrd (though only on ingested toxins), so that's not off the table there either. So 2e seems to have supernatural anti-toxin powers acknowledge the nastier 2e rules via having direct decrease in Toxicity rather than bonus dice in a few places.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bonerstorm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144742#post1144742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, higher rolls aren't *completely* useless... but the 2E system makes them worth a lot less.</div> </div> </div> </div>Only if the goal is &quot;no damage&quot; not &quot;survival.&quot; And that's still technically the case in your house rule, because you keep what makes higher dice pools less effective in 2e: number of successes matters. You're just changing the math to be less severe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That means there's a real incentive to invest in merits like Hardy and that things like Basilisk's Touch are somewhat less win button. </div> </div> </div>There's already incentive to invest in Hardy (esp. since in collapses what used to be a bunch of Merits into one): not taking more damage. Every die increases your odds of taking less damage.<br /> <br /> And Basilisk's Touch not being a &quot;win&quot; button is pointless, because Atavisms are generally win buttons against anything that can't supernaturally resist what they do; esp. at Low Satiety. That's sort of the point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> medical treatment doesn't give bonuses vs poison beyond +1/+2 </div> </div> </div>Says what?<br /> <br /> The books don't really go into treating toxins with Medicine, but in the disease section they can mitigate the equivalent penalty (which is a huge deal to surviving), and at least one book has proper medical treatment halving the number of checks you have to make (which is a gigantic boon to survival).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> you can't heal poison damage while still suffering the ailment. </div> </div> </div>Again, said where? Environmental exposure says this, but Disease, Drugs, and Poisons don't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you really dive into the sheet, you'll see the main effect of 2E poison rules is that 3-5 Tox poisons are death sentences for most players and 6+ poisons are death sentences for maxed-out demigods. My fix retains the deadliness of poison, while making sure that resistance scales because of the flat damage curve. </div> </div> </div>Except this isn't actually valid. The number of rolls you make, and the period between rolls is completely up to the ST. A 9L toxin isn't killing a maxed out human character period if you only roll once. 3-5 is really nasty <b>if</b> it's a lot of rolls over a relatively short period of time. The longer the interval between checks, and the less of them, the easier they are to survive.<br /> <br /> As well, this is a game that gets very lethal for humans at the high end of things. And that's true in most places. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Final note: the fix does NOT make poison less deadly than 1E. It increases lethality by 4% on average and is over twice as lethal at resist dice pools of 11+. It only makes high Tox poisons slightly less deadly for mid-range characters because they can shave a point or two off of damage instead of just rolling chance dies (which I think is more fun anyway). </div> </div> </div>So... it does exactly what I said it does?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bonerstorm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144745#post1144745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regardless: in my fix rules nobody's walking away unscathed from a Tox 7 heroin overdose (like 1E) nor do they get instagibbed by binging on Keystone Ice (2E). It just means that tougher characters have slightly more time to get Narcan or experience real body wreckage if they don't get their stomach pumped.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're still overstating things. Overdoses does intervals of hours, Toxicity 3 is going to take at least 3 hours to incapacitate an average person. That's... plenty of time to get to an ER unless you're somewhere very remote. Nobody is getting &quot;instagibbed&quot; by an 8 hour alcohol binge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bonerstorm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1144637-proposed-fix-for-2e-toxicity?p=1144749#post1144749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see a lot of &quot;just use grave poisoned tilt&quot; on the forums when people talk about Toxicity in 2E and that really drives home for me that RAW is broken.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quantity doesn't really equate to correctness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}